# Scenario bench-lamp-input (plan 08-18, D-23, D-39): a benchmark workload, not a test of behaviour. The same program, boot stub, zero
# graphics ROM and run length as bench-lamp-idle (Lamp Tone, 120 frames), with 64 input events, the scenario cap. The first six input
# lines are the six events of demo-lamp-tone-pass character for character, so that prefix is anchored by the hand-derived asserts of
# that scenario (data/golden/demo-lamp-tone-pass.expect); the other 58 are self-recorded repeatability only: from tick 820,000 at a
# spacing of 110,000 ticks, alternating the six controller word pairs of the pass scenario in order, each event moved by the smallest
# amount that puts it at least 16,800 ticks from a frame boundary of 59,736 ticks (the settling rule of
# docs/video/demo-lamp-tone-1-derivation.md), all before the last bound. It has no assert and no capture: its only oracle is the
# self-recorded repeatability golden data/golden/bench-lamp-input.digests (tier deterministic-repeatability), which tools/bench/bench.c
# compares with its own report before it reads a clock. A throughput figure printed for it is never a claim.
ivss 1
id bench-lamp-input
profile intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1
content exec boot-stub-2
content grom zero-grom
content cart lamp-tone.ivsc
input at_tick=100000 c0=KEY_1 c1=0x0
input at_tick=220000 c0=KEY_1+KEY_9 c1=0x0
input at_tick=340000 c0=0x0 c1=DISC_E
input at_tick=460000 c0=SIDE_TOP c1=DISC_E
input at_tick=580000 c0=0x0 c1=0x0
input at_tick=700000 c0=KEY_1+KEY_9 c1=SIDE_LOWER_LEFT
